@node emacsctl Overview
@section emacsctl Overview

@command{emacsctl} is cmacs's external control plane.  Every cmacs
subsystem reachable over the typed D-Bus interfaces --- buffers,
windows, projects, the gowl compositor, crispy, bacon, eshell, the
AI subsystem, gsurf, gnuseye, podomation, video/audio/speech, the
libregnum editor, cintrospect/cpatch --- is a first-class command.

Design pillars:

@table @strong
@item Scriptable
Every command takes @option{-o table|json|yaml|raw}.  @code{table}
is the human default; @code{raw} is byte-exact for shell
substitution; @code{json} / @code{yaml} parse cleanly.

@item Multi-instance
Every running cmacs claims @code{org.cmacs.Editor.Pid<N>} on the
session bus.  @code{emacsctl instances} enumerates them;
@option{--instance <pid>} targets one; by default the @emph{newest}
running instance is selected automatically (@option{--instance
primary} pins the well-known-name holder instead).

@item Multi-language
@code{eval} and @code{repl} speak elisp, crispy (inline C), bacon
(shell), and eshell --- all evaluated @emph{inside} the target
editor.  The language layer is an abstract runtime registry.

@item Remote
@option{--host user@@machine} tunnels everything (calls @emph{and}
live signal streams) over a single ssh connection.

@item Contexts
A kubeconfig-style file (@file{~/.config/cmacs/emacsctl.yaml}) names
instance/host/output combinations; @option{--context laptop}
switches everything at once.
@end table

@subsection When to use what

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .35 .45
@headitem Tool @tab Reaches @tab Best at
@item @command{emacsctl}
@tab any cmacs, from anywhere (incl. ssh)
@tab scripting, ops, REPLs, watching, multi-instance
@item @command{cmacsgi}
@tab the parent cmacs, from a bacon child
@tab in-shell editor calls while you work in bacon
@item @command{emacsclient}
@tab the Emacs server
@tab opening files, @env{EDITOR} protocol
@item @command{gdbus}
@tab any cmacs
@tab one-off raw D-Bus pokes, debugging the service
@item MCP socket
@tab one cmacs
@tab AI agents speaking the MCP protocol
@end multitable

emacsctl and the MCP server expose the @emph{same} capability set by
design: every MCP tool has a typed D-Bus method (the ``MCP parity''
interfaces), and emacsctl wraps those methods.

@subsection Exit codes

@table @code
@item 0
success
@item 1
generic or remote error
@item 2
usage error (unknown command, bad arguments)
@item 3
interface missing on the server (older cmacs, or subsystem not
compiled in)
@item 4
no instance found / selector ambiguous or invalid
@end table
